{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1777827110,"nonce":"prYgg9Kh0kgqK09kAyf4EcAzgT8=","title":"CI: Windows MSVC build broken since 2026-03-12 (pre-existing)","message":"Every commit since 9525a81 (Phase 2 compression engine) fails the Windows MSVC build job. Linux and macOS jobs pass. The Windows job has been red on every run for 7+ weeks; the failure is pre-existing relative to Phase 7 / license audit / writeup work in this session.\n\nTwo compile errors:\n\n1. cli/src/main.c:33: 'dirent.h' not found.\n   Added by 9525a81 for the directory-scanner (scan_dir) needed by archive creation. MSVC has no dirent.h.\n   Fix: add a minimal dirent.h shim under cli/src/compat/include/msvc/. Several well-known portable shims exist (~300 lines, public-domain).\n\n2. cli/src/compat/compat_win32.c:314: 'utimbuf' redefinition.\n   The compat layer defines utimbuf, but MSVC's sys/utime.h (transitively pulled in via direct.h / io.h) already defines it. The Win10 SDK in CI exposes the conflict; older SDKs may not have.\n   Fix: drop the local utimbuf definition and pull \u003csys/utime.h\u003e, OR guard the local definition behind #ifndef _UTIMBUF_DEFINED.\n\nBoth fixes are small. Bundling them with a CI re-run would close out a long-standing Windows-port regression and unblock the public-release Windows binary.","files":null}]}